<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Petrarch</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1304">1304</a>–1374</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Francesco Petrarca</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Petrarch, Francesco Petrarca, was an Italian poet and scholar born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arezzo">Arezzo</a> in 1304. His Italian love poems to Laura, collected in the Canzoniere, shaped European lyric poetry for centuries, and his search for classical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> made him a founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> humanism. Crowned poet laureate in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1341, he spent his last years near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a> under the patronage of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carrara">Carrara</a> lords.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/petrarch.jpg" alt="Petrarch" width="320" height="403" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Francesco_Petrarca_(called_Petrarch)_-_French_school,_17th_century.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Petrarch was born on 20 July 1304, the son of Ser Petracco and his wife Eletta Canigiani. His birth name was Francesco di Petracco, which he Latinized to Franciscus Petrarcha. His family followed Pope Clement V to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a>, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> had moved in 1309. His father, a notary, insisted that he and his brother Gherardo study law, at Montpellier from 1316 to 1320 and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a> from 1320 to 1326. Petrarch preferred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> literature and thought the seven years wasted. His father once threw his books into a fire.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After their parents died, Petrarch and his brother returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a> in 1326, where he held clerical offices that left time for writing. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> epic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, about the Roman general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-africanus">Scipio Africanus</a>, made him a European celebrity. On 8 April 1341 he was crowned on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitoline-hill">Capitoline Hill</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> by the senators Giordano Orsini and Orso dell'Anguillara, the second poet laureate since classical antiquity. He travelled widely and served as an ambassador.</p><p>He collected crumbling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> manuscripts, and in 1345 he found in the Chapter Library of Verona Cathedral a collection of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a>'s letters not known to have survived, the Epistulae ad Atticum. About 1368 he moved with his daughter Francesca and her family to Arquà in the Euganean Hills near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a>, where he spent his remaining years in religious contemplation. The da <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carrara">Carrara</a> lords of Padua seized his library, and its manuscripts are now scattered across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Petrarch is best known for the Rerum vulgarium fragmenta, or Canzoniere, a collection of 366 poems, 317 of them sonnets. The poems centre on Laura, whom he said he first saw in the church of Sainte-Claire d'<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a> on 6 April 1327. She may have been Laura de Noves, though he always denied that she was an invented figure. The Petrarchan sonnet still bears his name, and in the 16th century Pietro Bembo made his verse a model for the Italian language.</p><p>Most of his writing was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, including the Secretum, an imagined dialogue with a figure inspired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustine-of-hippo">Augustine of Hippo</a>, and a large body of letters. He valued the study of ancient history and literature as the study of human thought and action, and is often called the father of humanism. He is credited with creating the idea of a historical &quot;Dark Ages&quot;, a notion most modern scholars find inaccurate and misleading.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Petrarch” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The lyric of fin'amor that the troubadours developed spread to northern France, Germany, Italy and Iberia and shaped European love poetry, from the trouvères and Minnesänger to Dante and <b>Petrarch</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-aquitaine">Duchy of Aquitaine · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Cultural life flourished at court and in the capital, from Giotto and <b>Petrarch</b> under Robert the Wise to the humanists of Alfonso V, the painting of Caravaggio and Luca Giordano, and the Neapolitan school of opera in the eighteenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-naples">Kingdom of Naples · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Frederick II, known as stupor mundi, wrote a treatise on falconry and patronised the Sicilian School of poets, whose love lyrics in a Sicilian vernacular produced the sonnet, usually credited to Giacomo da Lentini, and influenced Dante and <b>Petrarch</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sicily">Kingdom of Sicily · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Dante’s Divine Comedy, together with the works of <b>Petrarch</b> and Giovanni Boccaccio, made the Tuscan dialect of Florence the basis of literary Italian.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-florence">Republic of Florence · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The humanists of the Renaissance, beginning with <b>Petrarch</b>, sought to recover the language of Cicero and wrote a revived classical Latin, now called Neo-Latin.</blockquote><a href="/language/latin">Latin · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>Some historians therefore trace the beginnings of humanism to Padua rather than to <b>Petrarch</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/renaissance-humanism">Renaissance humanism · Background</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arezzo">Arezzo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Arquà Petrarca, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.46,11.88&amp;year=1349" aria-label="Arezzo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4836 622 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Petrarch"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5286 892h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5286 892h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.46,11.88&amp;year=1349">What was here in 1349? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;It is possible that some word of me may have come to you, though even this is doubtful, since an insignificant and obscure name will scarcely penetrate far in either time or space.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to Posterity &#183; tr. James Harvey Robinson and Henry Winchester Rolfe, 1898 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48776/pg48776-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Among the many subjects which interested me, I dwelt especially upon antiquity, for our own age has always repelled me…&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to Posterity &#183; tr. James Harvey Robinson and Henry Winchester Rolfe, 1898 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48776/pg48776-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;To-day I made the ascent of the highest mountain in this region, which is not improperly called Ventosum.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter on the ascent of Mont Ventoux &#183; tr. James Harvey Robinson and Henry Winchester Rolfe, 1898 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48776/pg48776-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The greatest kings of this age have loved and courted me. They may know why; I certainly do not.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to Posterity &#183; tr. James Harvey Robinson and Henry Winchester Rolfe, 1898 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48776/pg48776-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I have always possessed an extreme contempt for wealth; not that riches are not desirable in themselves, but because I hate the anxiety and care which are invariably associated with them.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to Posterity &#183; tr. James Harvey Robinson and Henry Winchester Rolfe, 1898 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48776/pg48776-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I have taken pride in others, never in myself, and however insignificant I may have been, I have always been still less important in my own judgment. My anger has very often injured myself, but never others.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to Posterity &#183; tr. James Harvey Robinson and Henry Winchester Rolfe, 1898 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48776/pg48776-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
